WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
KASLIN FIELDS: Today,
we'll talk about one

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:05.670
aspect of running your
applications in Kubernetes,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.140
autoscaling.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.390
As the popularity of
Kubernetes has grown,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.240
the community isn't the
only thing that's scaled up.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.630
You also need to think about how
your applications will scale.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.290
Today, we'll look at autoscaling
your applications in GKE.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.900
I'm Kaslin Fields, and I'm
a Developer Advocate here

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.350
at Google Cloud, where I focus
on Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.620
or GKE, as well as
open-source Kubernetes.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.590
And today, we're going to take
a closer look at the autoscaling

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.270
capabilities of GKE.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.595
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.410
At some point, you've
probably been in a situation

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.840
where you've had to
think about bin packing.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.360
Bin packing is the
concept of packing items

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.720
inside a confined space in
order to use the space most

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.100
efficiently.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.980
The same concept applies when
you're running applications

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.420
on infrastructure.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.880
You want to use your
infrastructure resources

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.580
you're paying for,
such as CPU and memory,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.019
as efficiently as possible.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.090
For example, here's a diagram
showing a Google Cloud N1

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:13.340
standard 16 compute instance
with 16 vCPUs and 64 gigs

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.290
of memory.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.410
The boxes coming from the
bottom left of the graph

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:19.970
represent workloads.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.460
You can see that around
12.5 vCPU of the 16 vCPU

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.140
and 28.5 gigabytes out
of the 64 gigabytes

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.840
are utilized by these workloads.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.130
This means that there are about
3 vCPU and over 30 gigabytes

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:41.000
of RAM on this machine that
are not being utilized.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.120
Running machines that use these
resources more efficiently

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.580
would help you to
optimize your cost,

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.390
but figuring out which workloads
will be run on which machines,

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.690
and thus managing the
utilization of your resources,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.570
can be quite challenging.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.460
Wouldn't it be easier
if the platform you're

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.530
running your application
on could simply

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.840
detect its required
resource utilization

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.130
and adjust accordingly?

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.760
This is the concept
behind autoscaling.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.830
When using Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.170
there are four
types, or dimensions,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.900
of autoscaling that
you should consider--

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.190
Horizontal Pod Autoscaling, or
HPA, Vertical Pod Autoscaling,

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:24.530
or VPA, Cluster Autoscaler,
CA, and Node Auto Provisioner,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.630
or NAP.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:31.940
This diagram illustrates
these scalability dimensions.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.710
On the top, you'll
see autoscalers

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.850
that relate to scaling your
workloads or Kubernetes pods.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.980
On the bottom, you'll
see autoscalers

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.970
that relate to scaling your
infrastructure or nodes.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.820
The horizontal pod autoscaler
and cluster autoscaler,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.070
which are both on the
left of the diagram,

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.560
both deal with
increasing or decreasing

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.300
the number of something.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.210
In the case of HPA,
that would mean

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.300
increasing the number of
pods running your workload

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.240
to meet demand, or
potentially decreasing

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.510
the number of pods running your
workload when demand is low.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.550
The cluster autoscaler
can be configured

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:11.120
to increase the number of nodes
in your cluster to meet demand

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.970
or, conversely, to reduce the
number of nodes in your cluster

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.020
so you're using your resources
most efficiently during times

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.210
of low demand.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.190
On the right side
of the diagram are

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.790
autoscalers which change
the size of something.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.150
The Vertical Pod Autoscaler,
or VPA, on the top

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.480
can help you set the best
resource requests and limits

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.050
for the workloads you're
running in Kubernetes pods.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.570
The node autoprovisioner
on the bottom

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.150
can select Compute
instance sizes

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.360
in order to most efficiently
pack your workloads based

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.260
on resource utilization.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.780
Together, these four
dimensions of autoscaling

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.960
provide tools that enable you
to run both the infrastructure

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.220
of your workloads, as well
as the workloads themselves,

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.310
on your Google Kubernetes
Engine clusters efficiently,

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:06.050
with minimal configuration as
your clusters needs change.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.750
It's also important to
mention that Google Kubernetes

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.900
Engine recently introduced
a new type of autoscaler

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.680
called the multidimensional
pod autoscaler.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.750
As the name implies,
the new tool

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.290
combines the capabilities of
the horizontal and vertical pod

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.810
autoscalers in order
to provide a more

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.270
streamlined and
easier-to-use method

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.490
to optimize the efficiency of
your workloads running on GKE.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.220
Your usage of these FOUR
autoscaling dimensions

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.050
will be a little
different depending

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:39.710
on whether you're using Google
Kubernetes Engine in a Standard

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.170
or Autopilot mode.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.110
We dive deeper into the
differences between these modes

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.550
in GKE Essentials--

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.110
Cluster Modes.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:52.580
Be sure to check out that
video for more details.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.590
While these various
types of autoscalers

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:58.190
can save you time and cost,
configuring and understanding

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:01.310
all of the autoscalers is
still quite a bit to remember.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:05.060
GKE Autopilot mode provides
a more hands-off approach

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.310
by configuring the
infrastructure components

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.590
of cluster autoscaling for you.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:13.910
This means that when running
Autopilot mode GKE clusters,

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.010
you would want to
focus on configuring

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.530
any of the pod
autoscaling options,

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.620
and you would not need to worry
about the cluster autoscaler

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.700
dimensions.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.850
These autoscaling tools can
help make your work easier

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.140
and more efficient.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.430
They can also help you
optimize your costs

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.940
so that you're using the
resources you're paying for

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.500
most efficiently.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.988
Here are some
additional resources

00:05:34.988 --> 00:05:36.530
which you can find
in the description

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.690
below that can
help you learn more

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.010
about optimizing your costs
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.900
The four dimensions
of autoscaling

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.630
in Google Kubernetes Engine
play an important role

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.430
when configuring
your workloads to run

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.680
efficiently on Kubernetes.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.618
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:54.160
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.460
to building a GKE cluster
that suits your needs.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.520
You can get started with GKE
by visiting the Google Cloud

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.800
Console and checking
out the links below.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.500
And stay tuned for our next
videos, where we dive deeper

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.520
into GKE topics.

